  <language>en_US</language>
  <keyword>isoutlier</keyword>
  <short_description>Find outliers in numeric data.</short_description>
  <syntax>
    <syntax_item>TF = isoutlier(A)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>TF = isoutlier(A, method)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>TF = isoutlier(A, 'percentiles', threshold)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>TF = isoutlier(A, movmethod, window)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>TF = isoutlier(..., dim)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>TF = isoutlier(..., Name, Value)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>[TF, L, U, C] = isoutlier(...)</syntax_item>
  </syntax>
  <description>
    <p><b>isoutlier</b> returns a logical array that marks elements detected as outliers.</p>
    <p>The supported methods are <b>median</b>, <b>mean</b>, <b>quartiles</b>, <b>percentiles</b>, <b>grubbs</b>, <b>gesd</b>, <b>movmedian</b>, and <b>movmean</b>. Numeric <b>NaN</b> values are omitted from threshold estimation and are not marked as outliers.</p>
    <p>Name-value arguments include <b>ThresholdFactor</b>, <b>MaxNumOutliers</b>, and <b>SamplePoints</b>. The additional outputs contain the lower threshold, upper threshold, and center value.</p>
  </description>
  <examples>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description />
      <example_item_data runnable="cli"><![CDATA[A = [57 59 60 100 59 58 57 58 300 61 62 60 62 58 57];
TF = isoutlier(A)]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description />
      <example_item_data runnable="cli"><![CDATA[A = [60 59 49 49 58 100 61 57 48 58];
[TF, L, U, C] = isoutlier(A, 'median')]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description />
      <example_item_data runnable="cli"><![CDATA[A = [1 1 100 1 1];
t = [1 2 100 101 102];
TF = isoutlier(A, 'movmedian', 3, 'SamplePoints', t)]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
  </examples>
